    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.git\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.11.x\n\n### Description\n\nAccording to the [documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-status#_background_refresh), `git status ...` will lock the index by default, which may cause other commands or external tools like lazygit to fail. This issue is especially noticeable during interactive rebasing. An appropriate solution is adding the `--no-optional-locks` option, which is also recommended in the mentioned documentation:\n\n> When `status` is run in the background, the lock held during the write may conflict with other simultaneous processes, causing them to fail. Scripts running `status` in the background should consider using `git --no-optional-locks status` (see [git[1]](https://git-scm.com/docs/git) for details).\n\n### Reproduction\n\nThe minimal configuration to reproduce:\n\n```lua\n-- Clone latest 'mini.nvim' (requires Git CLI installed)\nvim.cmd('echo \"Installing `mini.nvim`\" | redraw')\nlocal mini_path = vim.fn.stdpath('data') .. '/site/pack/deps/start/mini.nvim'\nlocal clone_cmd = { 'git', 'clone', '--depth=1', 'https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim', mini_path }\nvim.fn.system(clone_cmd)\nvim.cmd('echo \"`mini.nvim` is installed\" | redraw')\n\n-- Make sure 'mini.nvim' is available\nvim.cmd('packadd mini.nvim')\nrequire('mini.deps').setup()\n\n-- Add extra setup steps needed to reproduce the behavior\n-- Use `MiniDeps.add('user/repo')` to install another plugin from GitHub\n\nif vim.env['MINI_GIT_DISABLE'] == nil then require('mini.git').setup() end\n```\n\nA handy script to trigger index races:\n\n```sh\ntest_file=\"nvim-repro/test.txt\"\n# Open the test file with nvim to tell MiniGit to watch it.\nNVIM_APPNAME=nvim-repro nvim\n# Then continuously update the index in background.\nwhile true; do\n    for _ in $(seq 10); do echo \"$RANDOM\"; done >\"$test_file\"\n    git add \"$test_file\"\n    sleep 0.05\ndone\n```\n\nYou can find all the snippets above [here](https://github.com/loichyan/mini.nvim/commit/bd08ea889d51f97faf7be296cc5927dd6bec5502). I have verified the suggested solution as well. Running Neovim with either `MINI_GIT_DISABLE=1` or `MINI_GIT_NOLOCK=1` seems to resolve this issue.",
